A right shift moves the required bit to position zero. After shifting by `i`, checking `& 1` tells whether the bit is `0` or `1`.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean checkIthBit(int n, int i) {
        return ((n >> i) & 1) == 1;
    }
}
```

Dry run: `n = 13` is `1101` in binary. For `i = 2`, `n >> 2` becomes `0011`. The last bit is `1`, so the method returns `true`.

This reads the bit without changing the input number. The index is zero-based, so `i = 0` checks the rightmost bit.

Time complexity: `O(1)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

Instead of using a bitwise mask, divide by `2^i` first. This moves the required bit to the units place. Taking modulo `2` then gives its value.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool checkIthBit(int n, int i) {
        long long powerOfTwo = 1LL << i;
        return (n / powerOfTwo) % 2 == 1;
    }
};
```

For `n = 20` (`10100`) and `i = 2`, `20 / 4` is `5`, and `5 % 2` is `1`. Therefore, bit `2` is set.

The input is not modified. This assumes the usual problem condition that `n` is non-negative and `i` is a valid bit position.

Time complexity: `O(1)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

Each bit has a place value that is a power of two. To test bit `i`, find the value of `2^i` and see whether that value is present in the matching block of the number.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean checkIthBit(int n, int i) {
        long placeValue = 1L << i;
        long block = n / placeValue;
        return block % 2 == 1;
    }
}
```

For instance, `n = 9` is `1001`. At `i = 0`, the place value is `1`, and `9 % 2` is `1`, so the lowest bit is set. At `i = 1`, `9 / 2` is `4`, whose remainder by `2` is `0`.

A `long` is used for the power value so the shift does not overflow as quickly as an `int` shift.

Time complexity: `O(1)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The bit at position `i` can be selected with `1 << i`. If the result of `n & (1 << i)` is not zero, that bit was set in `n`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool checkIthBit(int n, int i) {
        int mask = 1 << i;
        return (n & mask) != 0;
    }
};
```

For example, with `n = 10` (`1010`) and `i = 1`, the mask is `0010`. The AND result is `0010`, so the answer is `true`.

Time complexity: `O(1)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`
